// _tests_/bit/fmtcitations/fmtcitationssrc.bit — the SOURCE-COMMENT citation
// scan (#4257): widens this gate beyond the three curated DocRow documents
// (fmtcitations.bit's docRows()) to `//` comments inside compiler/**,
// runtime/**, tools/build/** and _tests_/bit/** .bit source files
// themselves. #4246's manual sweep found 61 genuinely stale file citations
// in exactly this population, invisible to every existing gate (the three
// DocRow documents cover none of it) — this closes that gap mechanically,
// for the checkable subset only: a backtick-quoted bare symbol immediately
// preceding a `<file>.bit` token, verified against the file it names really
// declaring that symbol. Reuses checkSymbolCitation/checkSymbolMoved from
// fmtcitationsassoc.bit (sibling in this module, same Tier 2 logic the
// DocRow rows already use) by building a synthetic one-row DocRow per source
// file (docPath: the source file itself) — "the same shape ... already
// does ... only the input file list changes" (#4257).
//
// DELIBERATELY OUT OF SCOPE, and why:
//  - line-number bounds for a bare `path.bit:NNN` inside a source comment.
//    #4246 found this project actively DROPS line numbers from source
//    citations on every genuine fix ("the fastest-rotting citation there
//    is") rather than updating them, so most live citations here are
//    already bare; checking residual `:NNN` ones needs its own triage
//    pass, not this ticket's. A `.md:NNN` target is the opposite case and
//    IS checked -- see the next paragraph.
//  - a bare filename mention with NO adjacent symbol (e.g. "see build.bit
//    for the CLI entry point"). #4246 classed the bulk of its 263 raw grep
//    hits (155 of 263) as "still true" prose naming no specific symbol —
//    checking mere existence of every such mention adds no signal a stale
//    symbol claim doesn't already give, and it is exactly the shape that
//    would misfire on a `BIT_FMTZERO_DEBT=...` file-list string. That
//    string lives inside a `return "..."` statement, not a comment, so
//    scanning `//` lines only (see extractCommentBlocks) already keeps it
//    out of scope structurally, without a special case naming it.
//
// A `<doc>.md:NNN` TARGET IS BOUNDS-CHECKED (#4653), unlike the `.bit:NNN`
// case above. A source citation into a document is never dropped on a fix
// the way a citation into code is -- it is edited by whoever moves the
// prose, or more often not edited at all: the E0204 row in spec/LINT.md is
// cited by line number from 29 source comments across 24 files (2026-09-09),
// and lint-policy tickets edit that file routinely. Proved blind at
// e869af60: an injected 99999 in a tools/build comment left every counter
// unmoved and the gate at rc=0, because extractPathTokens is hardcoded to
// bitSuffix and nothing else looked at a source comment.
// checkSourceMdCitation runs the SAME Tier 1 the three DocRow documents run
// (checkBoundsShared) over the citations extractMdCitations
// (fmtcitationsmd.bit) already knows how to find -- no second bounds
// implementation. There is no Tier 2 here: a markdown line declares no
// symbol, so a symbol lookup has nothing to look up.
//
// WHAT REPLACES TIER 2 FOR THIS SHAPE (#4677): the citation's own quoted
// annotation. `spec/SPEC.md:70 ("if present as the first character")` is
// checked by reading line 70 and requiring it to CONTAIN that text
// (checkSourceMdQuote below). Bounds and content are both blind to the
// commonest drift there is -- five comments cited spec/SPEC.md:68 for a
// sentence that is at :70, and 68 is in range and carries an unrelated
// bullet -- so the only evidence left about WHICH line was meant is the text
// the comment itself quoted. Opt-in per citation: a bare citation still gets
// bounds and content only.
//
// A `.md` citation must name its REPO-RELATIVE PATH. There is no index and
// no basename fallback here, deliberately: resolveSourceToken exists
// because a `.bit` comment cites a SIBLING bare ("see fs.bit") and this
// repo has that basename four times over, but no `.md` file sits beside a
// `.bit` source file in any of the four roots, so a bare document basename
// has no "the one right here" reading to recover -- it is just an
// under-specified path, and it is reported as not existing under the repo
// root. #4653 repointed the three that existed (two in compiler/lower*.bit,
// one in compiler/arm64compile.bit) to their full paths.
//
// ONLY `//` COMMENT LINES ARE SCANNED. A maximal run of consecutive
// `//`-prefixed lines is one "comment block", extracted and scanned
// independently of every other block in the file — this is what keeps
// findAssociatedSymbol's own maxBackScan window from reaching across a
// blank line or a run of real code into an unrelated comment.
//
// THE "SAME MODULE AS X.bit: `a`, `b` are declared there" IDIOM (#4257's
// named trap; the real shape is _tests_/bit/golden/stage.bit:10 — "same
// module as golden.bit: `dRun`.. `dPanicDebug`, `Ctx`, `failCase` and
// `isRunLike` are declared there") cites the FILE before its symbol LIST.
// findAssociatedSymbol only ever associates a symbol that PRECEDES a
// citation (the doc-citation convention it was built for: `` `sym`
// (file.bit:N) ``), so this reversed idiom never reaches Tier 2 here at
// all — verified against the live corpus in this ticket's completion
// comment, not just reasoned about.
//
// A SECOND, MORE DANGEROUS same-module shape does reach Tier 2, and needed
// an explicit guard (hasPossessiveFileBefore): "fileA.bit's `sym1`/`sym2`,
// fileB.bit's `sym3`" — a comma-joined run of (file, its own symbols)
// pairs, e.g. compiler/pmz.bit:148's "(`pmcliaddref.bit`'s
// `addLocalDependency`, `pmcliup.bit`'s `upDependencies`)". Each symbol
// group already belongs to the file whose name IMMEDIATELY PRECEDES it via
// "'s" — but findAssociatedSymbol's chain logic (built for the DocRow
// shape, where ALL citations trail ALL their symbols) doesn't know that,
// and reassigns the group to the NEXT ".bit" token in sequence instead —
// pairing `addLocalDependency` (genuinely in pmcliaddref.bit) with
// `pmcliup.bit`, a false "moved" report. Guarded by refusing the BACKWARD
// rule whenever a "<file>.bit's" possessive appears in the backward-scan
// window — this is the repo's single most common comment idiom for citing a
// sibling's declarations (2365 possessive tokens across 522 files,
// 2026-09-09).
//
// THAT GUARD LEFT THE WHOLE IDIOM UNCHECKED, which is what #4682 fixes:
// both possessive guards are correct about the BACKWARD rule and say nothing
// about the forward one, so for two tickets every "x.bit's `sym`" citation
// in the repo got no Tier 2 at all. The measured cost: _tests_/bit/
// lowerrefuse.bit:106 named the wrong owner for `lowerIndex` — it cited
// compiler/lowerlit.bit, while the declaration is at
// compiler/loweraccess.bit:487 — and the gate exited 0.
// checkPossessiveCitation below reads the symbols AFTER the "'s"
// (possessiveSymbolsAfter, fmtcitationsassoc.bit) and verifies them against
// the file that owns it — a second, disjoint association rule, never a
// widening of the first: the guards stay exactly as they are.
//
// The census that sized it, over the whole live corpus on 7c6a13f0
// (2026-09-09; 806 `.bit` files under sourceRoots()): 2365 possessive
// tokens, 958 of them followed by a backtick-quoted bare identifier, 1049
// (file, symbol) pairs once chained lists are expanded. 89 pairs never reach
// a check — 61 whose file does not resolve, 28 naming a provider FAMILY —
// leaving 960 checked: 716 verified, 41 reported, and 203 deliberately
// silent (176 "the symbol is still in the cited file's text", 16 "declared
// nowhere", 11 ambiguous), each stated where it is implemented. Every one of
// the 41 was read by hand and every one was a genuine stale citation: 0
// false positives, on a denominator of 1049.

const maxCommentBlockChars: int = 65536
const maxSourceFiles: int = 1024
const maxDoubleBacktickMarkers: int = 64

// A floor, not a pinned count (#4682): 716 of the 960 (file, symbol) pairs
// the possessive rule associates verified on 7c6a13f0, and a comment edit
// moves that number every week. Set well under it so a regression toward
// zero — the guard silently swallowing the whole idiom again, which is
// exactly what this ticket found — fails loudly, while an ordinary
// add/delete never trips it. Same reasoning as minTotalCitationsFloor.
const minPossessivePairedFloor: int = 500

fn sourceRoots(): []string {
  return ["compiler", "runtime", "tools/build", "_tests_/bit"]
}

class CommentBlock { text: string, startLine: int }

fn isCommentLine(line: string): bool {
  return hasPrefix(trimWs(line), "//")
}

// `line`'s own "//" marker replaced by two spaces, its length unchanged
// (#4288). LENGTH-PRESERVING is the whole point: every offset a scanner
// reports — `PathToken.startOffset`, `docLineFor`'s newline count, the
// `text[a:b]` slices every backward scan takes — indexes into the joined
// block text, so a substitution that moved characters would shift every
// citation's reported line and column. Two spaces for two slashes moves
// nothing.
fn blankCommentMarker(line: string): string {
  let n = len(line)
  let i = 0
  while (i < n && (line[i] == ' ' || line[i] == '\t')) {
    i = i + 1
  }
  if (i + 2 > n || line[i:i + 2] != "//") {
    return line
  }
  return line[0:i] + "  " + line[i + 2:n]
}

// Every maximal run of consecutive `//`-prefixed lines in `lines`, each
// joined by "\n" with its 1-based starting line number in the file it came
// from — never crosses a non-comment line. Each line's own "//" marker is
// blanked on the way in (blankCommentMarker above).
//
// #4288: the marker has to go HERE, once, rather than in each scanner that
// reads across the join. A backtick-quoted symbol closing one line and its
// `(file.bit)` opening the next left `backtickSpanBefore` a connector of
// "// (", which `isEntryConnector` rejects, so the citation associated with
// nothing and was silently never checked — the same silence #4285 fixed for
// the bare-filename case, from a different cause. Every line inside a block
// is a comment line by construction, so blanking is total: no scanner below
// has to know a line boundary is there, and `skipCommentWrapWs`
// (fmtcitationsassoc.bit) drops from "step over a wrap and its marker" to
// plain whitespace skipping.
fn extractCommentBlocks(lines: []string): []CommentBlock {
  let out = []CommentBlock(0)
  let n = len(lines)
  let i = 0
  while (i < n) {
    if (!isCommentLine(lines[i])) {
      i = i + 1
      continue
    }
    let start = i
    let buf = blankCommentMarker(lines[i])
    i = i + 1
    while (i < n && isCommentLine(lines[i]) && len(buf) < maxCommentBlockChars) {
      buf = buf + "\n" + blankCommentMarker(lines[i])
      i = i + 1
    }
    out = append(out, CommentBlock{ text: buf, startLine: start + 1 })
  }
  return out
}

// The (sc, root, index, path) bundle every helper below needs, threaded
// through as one value instead of four separate params — same reasoning as
// fmtcitationsassoc.bit's CheckCtx (E0202's 5-param limit).
class SourceFileCtx {
  sc: Scan, root: string, index: []string, declIndex: []string, path: string,
}

// How far back to look for a "<file>.bit's" possessive before trusting a
// symbol association — matches maxBackScan, the same window
// findAssociatedSymbol itself searches to find that association in the
// first place, so this guard only ever inspects text the pairing logic
// already considered relevant.
const possessiveScanWindow: int = 200

// The offset right after `tok`'s own raw ".bit" text in the SAME `text` it
// was extracted from. NOT `tok.startOffset + len(tok.file)`: `tok.file` is
// normalized (normalizePathTokenName strips a leading "/" left over from
// e.g. "./root.bit"), so that arithmetic undercounts by 1 whenever a token
// had one — found via `./root.bit's own` failing to match as a possessive.
// Searching forward for the literal ".bit" is exact because `tok` was
// itself produced by finding that same literal occurrence.
fn tokenRawEnd(text: string, tok: PathToken): int {
  let at = int(indexOfFrom(text, bitSuffix, tok.startOffset))
  if (at < 0) {
    return tok.startOffset + len(tok.file)
  }
  return at + len(bitSuffix)
}

// Whether `text[end:]` is "'s", allowing ONE closing backtick in between —
// a possessive file citation is routinely backtick-quoted itself
// ("`pmcliaddref.bit`'s `addLocalDependency`"), which puts the closing
// backtick between the filename text and the apostrophe.
fn followedByPossessive(text: string, end: int): bool {
  let p = end
  let n = len(text)
  if (p < n && text[p] == '`') {
    p = p + 1
  }
  return p + 2 <= n && text[p:p + 2] == "'s"
}

// Whether `text[max(0,at-possessiveScanWindow):at]` contains a `.bit` token
// immediately followed by "'s" — the "fileA.bit's `sym`" possessive idiom
// that findAssociatedSymbol's chain logic mis-attributes across file
// boundaries (see the header comment above).
fn hasPossessiveFileBefore(text: string, at: int): bool {
  let lo = at - possessiveScanWindow
  if (lo < 0) {
    lo = 0
  }
  if (at <= lo || at > len(text)) {
    return false
  }
  let window = text[lo:at]
  for tok of extractPathTokens(window, "") {
    let end = tokenRawEnd(window, tok)
    if (followedByPossessive(window, end)) {
      return true
    }
  }
  return false
}

// Every offset where a run of two adjacent backtick characters starts, in
// order, capped at maxDoubleBacktickMarkers. Markdown's escape convention
// for "this span itself contains a backtick" — this repo's own
// citation-scanning code uses it to give a GENERIC EXAMPLE of the citation
// shape it matches (see this file's own header comment above), where the
// example filenames are stand-ins, never real ones. Paired up by
// isInsideDoubleBacktickSpan below. NOTE: writing that two-backtick token
// itself as a quoted illustration (as this sentence just avoided doing) would
// be self-defeating — it would be counted as a marker and shift the parity
// of every real span later in the same comment block.
fn doubleBacktickPositions(text: string): []int {
  let out = []int(0)
  let n = len(text)
  let i = 0
  while (i < n && len(out) < maxDoubleBacktickMarkers) {
    let rel = indexOfFrom(text, "``", i)
    if (int(rel) < 0) {
      break
    }
    let pos = int(rel)
    out = append(out, pos)
    i = pos + 2
  }
  return out
}

// Whether `offset` falls strictly between an (odd, even)-indexed pair of
// `positions` — i.e. inside an OPEN..CLOSE `` `` ``-delimited span, treated
// as a literal illustrative example rather than a real citation.
fn isInsideDoubleBacktickSpan(positions: []int, offset: int): bool {
  let i = 0
  let n = len(positions)
  while (i + 1 < n) {
    if (offset > positions[i] && offset < positions[i + 1]) {
      return true
    }
    i = i + 2
  }
  return false
}

// `path`'s directory, or "" if it has none (a bare top-level file).
fn dirOf(path: string): string {
  let idx = int(lastIndexOf(path, "/"))
  if (idx < 0) {
    return ""
  }
  return path[0:idx]
}

// Resolves a citation the same way `resolveToken` does, EXCEPT: a bare
// basename (no "/" of its own) is tried first against the CITING file's own
// directory. Source comments routinely cite a sibling file bare ("same
// module as boot.bit", "see fs.bit") meaning "the one right here" — and this
// repo has the same basename (boot.bit, os.bit, fs.bit, sock.bit, tcp.bit,
// main.bit, verify.bit, gcyield.bit, ...) duplicated once per OS provider or
// build/runtime split, so the GLOBAL index sees 2-4 candidates and
// (correctly, per resolveToken's own ambiguity discipline) refuses to guess.
// A token that already names a directory (e.g. "linux/boot.bit") is a
// deliberate cross-reference and skips this — it goes straight to the
// global index, same as before.
//
// A citation WRITTEN with a "../" run is neither: it names a path relative to
// the citing file's own directory, and extractPathTokens cannot carry that —
// its backward scan stops at the '.', so "../os.bit" arrives here as the bare
// "os.bit" and the sibling-first rule above then resolves it to the citing
// file's OWN directory, one level too low. Measured wrong both ways on
// 2026-09-09 (#4682): runtime/root/linux/boottail.bit:558's "`../os.bit`'s
// `osMaxEnvCount`" was resolved to runtime/root/linux/os.bit and reported as
// a false "moved" (it is runtime/root/os.bit, which declares it), while
// runtime/root/windows/fs.bit:43's "`../os.bit`'s `winUtf8ToUtf16`" was
// resolved to runtime/root/windows/os.bit and silently PASSED — the citation
// is genuinely one level too high. So a ".." run is placed against the citing
// file's directory here, and a ".." that cannot be placed is unresolved
// rather than guessed at: this arm never falls back to a sibling it was
// explicitly told not to mean.
fn resolveSourceToken(fc: SourceFileCtx, text: string, tok: PathToken): (string, bool) {
  let prefix = relativePrefixBefore(text, tok.startOffset)
  if (contains(prefix, "..")) {
    return resolveRelativeToken(fc, prefix, tok.file)
  }
  if (!contains(tok.file, "/")) {
    let dir = dirOf(fc.path)
    let candidate = tok.file
    if (len(dir) > 0) {
      candidate = "${dir}/${tok.file}"
    }
    if (exists("${fc.root}/${candidate}")) {
      return candidate, true
    }
  }
  return resolveToken(fc.root, tok.file, fc.index, "")
}

// The run of '.' and '/' characters immediately before `at` — "" for an
// ordinary citation, "./" or "../" or "../../" for a relative one. This is
// exactly the text extractPathTokens' backward scan refused to take, so
// reading it back off the raw text is the only place it still exists.
fn relativePrefixBefore(text: string, at: int): string {
  let p = at
  if (p > len(text)) {
    return ""
  }
  while (p > 0 && (text[p - 1] == '.' || text[p - 1] == '/')) {
    p = p - 1
  }
  return text[p:at]
}

// `tokenFile` placed against the citing file's directory, one level up per
// ".." in `prefix`. Unresolved (never a guess) when the run climbs past the
// repo root or lands on no file.
fn resolveRelativeToken(fc: SourceFileCtx, prefix: string, tokenFile: string): (string, bool) {
  let base = dirOf(fc.path)
  let ups = countDotDot(prefix)
  let i = 0
  while (i < ups) {
    if (len(base) == 0) {
      return "", false
    }
    base = dirOf(base)
    i = i + 1
  }
  let candidate = tokenFile
  if (len(base) > 0) {
    candidate = "${base}/${tokenFile}"
  }
  if (!exists("${fc.root}/${candidate}")) {
    return "", false
  }
  return candidate, true
}

// The number of ".." segments in a relative prefix ("../../" is 2). `s` holds
// nothing but '.' and '/' by construction, so counting non-overlapping ".."
// pairs is exact.
fn countDotDot(s: string): int {
  let n = 0
  let i = 0
  while (i + 1 < len(s)) {
    if (s[i] == '.' && s[i + 1] == '.') {
      n = n + 1
      i = i + 2
      continue
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return n
}

// One symbol-adjacent citation found inside a comment block: resolves the
// cited file and runs the same Tier 2 check a DocRow row runs, reported
// against the SOURCE file's own path and real line number (not the row's
// nominal docPath, which is why a fresh single-row DocRow is built here
// instead of reusing one of docRows()'s three entries).
fn checkSourceToken(fc: SourceFileCtx, block: CommentBlock, tok: PathToken, dbPositions: []int) {
  if (isInsideDoubleBacktickSpan(dbPositions, tok.startOffset)) {
    return
  }
  // This token itself is the SUBJECT of a possessive ("x64call.bit's
  // `xArg0`") — any symbol findAssociatedSymbol pairs backward from here
  // belongs to an unrelated earlier clause, not to this citation. The
  // FORWARD rule (#4682) is the one that reads it: the symbols after the
  // "'s" are this file's own, and the backward path must still not run.
  if (followedByPossessive(block.text, tokenRawEnd(block.text, tok))) {
    checkPossessiveCitation(fc, block, tok)
    return
  }
  let symbolName = findAssociatedSymbol(block.text, tok.startOffset)
  if (len(symbolName) == 0) {
    return
  }
  if (hasPossessiveFileBefore(block.text, tok.startOffset)) {
    return
  }
  let actualLine = block.startLine + tok.docLine - 1
  let (resolved, ok) = resolveSourceToken(fc, block.text, tok)
  if (!ok) {
    fc.sc.citations = fc.sc.citations + 1
    report(
      fc.sc,
      "${fc.path}:${actualLine}: cites `${symbolName}` in ${tok.file}, which does not resolve to a file under any of: ${join(
        sourceRoots(),
        ", ",
      )}",
    )
    return
  }
  // `symbolName` is ALSO declared right here in the citing file itself, and
  // the cited file is a DIFFERENT one. No comment genuinely means "see file
  // Y for symbol S" when S is declared right here — this is a comparison or
  // a negation instead: this repo's own runtime/thread/linux/tls.bit:208
  // reads "our own linker emits no PT_PHDR, elflink.bit writes ... instead"
  // — a constant that PT_PHDR names is declared in tls.bit itself, at line
  // 83, not in the file the sentence names. checkSymbolMoved can't tell a
  // "declared elsewhere" claim from a "described elsewhere" one; refusing
  // whenever the citing file already has its own declaration is the cheap,
  // conservative half of that distinction we CAN make mechanically.
  if (resolved != fc.path && declaresSymbol(fc.root, fc.path, symbolName)) {
    return
  }
  fc.sc.citations = fc.sc.citations + 1
  verifySourceCitation(fc, resolved, actualLine, symbolName, tok)
}

// Reads the already-resolved cited file, bounds-checks it, and hands off to
// checkSymbolCitation — split out of checkSourceToken to keep it under
// E0204's complexity-10 limit.
fn verifySourceCitation(
  fc: SourceFileCtx,
  resolved: string,
  actualLine: int,
  symbolName: string,
  tok: PathToken,
) {
  let (lines, ok) = citedFileLines(fc, resolved, actualLine, symbolName)
  if (!ok) {
    return
  }
  checkSymbolCitation(
    sourceCheckCtx(fc),
    citedSymbolAt(resolved, tok, actualLine, symbolName),
    lines,
  )
}

// The cited file's lines, or ok=false when it could not be read (reported
// against the CITING file, as every finding in this arm is). Shared by the
// backward arm and the possessive one so a second read path cannot drift
// from this one's bounds.
fn citedFileLines(
  fc: SourceFileCtx,
  resolved: string,
  actualLine: int,
  symbolName: string,
): ([]string, bool) {
  let full = "${fc.root}/${resolved}"
  let text = readFile(full) catch e {
    report(
      fc.sc,
      "${fc.path}:${actualLine}: cites `${symbolName}` in ${resolved}, but it could not be read: ${e.message()}",
    )
    ""
  }
  if (len(text) == 0) {
    return []string(0), false
  }
  if (len(text) > maxCitedFileBytes) {
    die("${resolved} is ${len(text)} bytes, over the ${maxCitedFileBytes} bound")
  }
  let lines = split(text, "\n")
  if (len(lines) > maxCitedFileLines) {
    die("${resolved} has ${len(lines)} lines, over the ${maxCitedFileLines} bound")
  }
  return lines, true
}

// The synthetic one-row CheckCtx this arm reports against: docPath is the
// SOURCE file's own path, not one of docRows()'s three documents, which is
// why a fresh row is built per citation instead of reusing one of theirs.
fn sourceCheckCtx(fc: SourceFileCtx): CheckCtx {
  let row = DocRow{
    docPath: fc.path, searchRoots: sourceRoots(), requiredPrefix: "",
    checkBareMentions: false, verifySymbols: true, minCitations: 0,
  }
  let seen = SeenSymbols{ names: []string(0) }
  return CheckCtx{
    sc: fc.sc, root: fc.root, row: row, index: fc.index,
    declIndex: fc.declIndex, seen: seen,
  }
}

// `tok` re-stamped with the citing file's REAL line number (the block's
// start plus the token's line within it), which is the only number a reader
// can act on.
fn citedSymbolAt(
  resolved: string,
  tok: PathToken,
  actualLine: int,
  symbolName: string,
): CitedSymbol {
  return CitedSymbol{
    resolved: resolved,
    tok: PathToken{
      file: tok.file, lineSpecRaw: tok.lineSpecRaw, hasSpec: tok.hasSpec,
      docLine: actualLine, startOffset: tok.startOffset,
    },
    symbolName: symbolName,
  }
}

// The FORWARD arm (#4682): "<file>.bit's `sym1`/`sym2`" — every symbol after
// the possessive belongs to the file that owns the "'s", checked with
// checkPossessiveSymbol (fmtcitationsassoc.bit). Reached only from
// checkSourceToken's possessive guard, so the backward rule and this one can
// never both run on one token.
fn checkPossessiveCitation(fc: SourceFileCtx, block: CommentBlock, tok: PathToken) {
  let names = possessiveSymbolsAfter(block.text, tokenRawEnd(block.text, tok))
  if (len(names) == 0) {
    return
  }
  if (namesProviderFamily(block.text, tok.startOffset)) {
    return
  }
  let (resolved, ok) = resolveSourceToken(fc, block.text, tok)
  if (!ok) {
    return
  }
  let actualLine = block.startLine + tok.docLine - 1
  for name of names {
    fc.sc.citations = fc.sc.citations + 1
    verifyPossessiveSymbol(fc, resolved, actualLine, name, tok)
  }
}

// Whether the citation was cut out of a path naming a FAMILY of provider
// files rather than one file: "runtime/root/{darwin,linux}/boot.bit",
// "runtime/root/*/os.bit", "<os>/io.bit". extractPathTokens' backward scan
// stops at the '}', '>' or '*', so the token arrives as a bare basename and
// then resolves — wrongly, and silently — against whichever single provider
// happens to sit beside the citing file. 27 sites of the 958 (2026-09-09);
// in one, a comment in runtime/root/slices.bit citing `<os>/io.bit` for
// `ioConstBuf` resolved to runtime/root/io.bit, a file that never held it. A family names no one
// file, so there is nothing here to verify and nothing to report.
fn namesProviderFamily(text: string, at: int): bool {
  let p = at - len(relativePrefixBefore(text, at))
  if (p <= 0 || p > len(text)) {
    return false
  }
  let c = text[p - 1]
  return c == '}' || c == '>' || c == '*'
}

// One (file, symbol) pair of a possessive citation, checked against the
// cited file's own text.
fn verifyPossessiveSymbol(
  fc: SourceFileCtx,
  resolved: string,
  actualLine: int,
  symbolName: string,
  tok: PathToken,
) {
  let (lines, ok) = citedFileLines(fc, resolved, actualLine, symbolName)
  if (!ok) {
    return
  }
  checkPossessiveSymbol(
    sourceCheckCtx(fc),
    citedSymbolAt(resolved, tok, actualLine, symbolName),
    lines,
  )
}

// A `("...")` annotation written inside a `//` comment may cross a comment
// line wrap, which puts a newline, the next line's own "//" and its
// indentation INSIDE the quoted text — the docs arm never sees this because
// markdown wraps with a bare newline. Rejoining those into single spaces is
// what lets such a quote be compared against the one markdown line it names;
// without it a wrapped annotation could only ever be a false failure (or a
// silent skip, which is worse: the writer who bothered to quote the sentence
// is exactly the one who expects it checked).
fn unwrapCommentQuote(needle: string): string {
  let parts = split(needle, "\n")
  if (len(parts) < 2) {
    return needle
  }
  let out = trimWs(parts[0])
  let i = 1
  while (i < len(parts)) {
    let seg = trimWs(parts[i])
    if (hasPrefix(seg, "//")) {
      seg = trimWs(seg[2:len(seg)])
    }
    if (len(seg) > 0) {
      out = "${out} ${seg}"
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return out
}

// The QUOTE check (#4677) for one source-comment citation, run after bounds
// and content have both passed: a citation followed by a `("...")`
// annotation must name a line that CONTAINS the quoted text.
//
// Same annotation reader as the docs arm (headingAnnotationAfter), different
// predicate — lineContainsQuoted, not lineIsHeadingContaining. #4670
// proposed the heading predicate here and measured it wrong: the whole
// population of annotated source comments was one site, whose parenthetical
// quotes the cited SENTENCE, and a heading predicate fails it. A document
// names a section; a source comment quotes prose.
//
// Opt-in per citation, exactly as the docs arm's annotation is: a bare
// citation gets bounds and content only. That is the point of the shape —
// the writer who quotes the sentence is asking for the stronger check, and
// inventing a quote for an unannotated citation would be the guessing this
// module refuses everywhere else.
fn checkSourceMdQuote(fc: SourceFileCtx, blockText: string, q: SourceQuoteQuery) {
  let (raw, hasAnnotation) = headingAnnotationAfter(blockText, q.endOffset)
  if (!hasAnnotation) {
    return
  }
  let needle = unwrapCommentQuote(raw)
  if (!lineContainsQuoted(q.lines, q.lineNum, needle)) {
    report(
      fc.sc,
      "${fc.path}:${q.actualLine}: cites ${q.file}:${q.lineNum} as (\"${needle}\"), but that line does not contain that text — the citation has drifted or the quote is wrong",
    )
    return
  }
  fc.sc.quoteVerified = fc.sc.quoteVerified + 1
}

// The five values checkSourceMdQuote needs about one citation, bundled so it
// stays inside E0202's 5-parameter limit — the same reason SourceFileCtx and
// CheckCtx exist.
class SourceQuoteQuery {
  file: string, lineNum: int, actualLine: int, endOffset: int, lines: []string,
}

// Tier 1 for one `<doc>.md:NNN` citation in a source comment: the document
// exists at the path the comment names, every line number the spec names is
// within [1, its line count], and the FIRST of those lines carries content.
// `checkBoundsShared` and `lineHasCitableContent` are the same two the three
// DocRow documents' own citations go through, so a source comment and a
// document get one implementation of each, not two.
//
// The content check (#4670) is the cheapest thing that separates a live
// citation from one that has drifted onto a still-in-range line, which
// bounds passes forever. It is a floor: a drift onto another paragraph
// still passes — which is what the QUOTE check above (#4677) narrows, for
// the citations that opt into it. The measured instance it was built on:
// five comments cited spec/SPEC.md:68 for the byte order mark sentence,
// which is at spec/SPEC.md:70; bounds passed all five and so did content,
// because line 68 is an ordinary bullet about something else.
fn checkSourceMdCitation(
  fc: SourceFileCtx,
  block: CommentBlock,
  c: MdCitation,
  dbPositions: []int,
) {
  if (isInsideDoubleBacktickSpan(dbPositions, c.startOffset)) {
    return
  }
  let actualLine = block.startLine + c.docLine - 1
  fc.sc.citations = fc.sc.citations + 1
  let res = checkBoundsShared(
    fc.sc,
    fc.root,
    BoundsQuery{
      docPath: fc.path, docLine: actualLine, resolved: c.file,
      parseSpec: c.lineSpecRaw, displaySpec: c.lineSpecRaw, verb: "cites",
    },
  )
  if (!res.ok) {
    return
  }
  fc.sc.boundsChecked = fc.sc.boundsChecked + 1
  let lineNum = specFirstLine(c.lineSpecRaw)
  if (!lineHasCitableContent(res.lines, lineNum)) {
    report(
      fc.sc,
      "${fc.path}:${actualLine}: cites ${c.file}:${lineNum}, but that line is blank or a code-fence marker — the citation has drifted",
    )
    return
  }
  fc.sc.contentChecked = fc.sc.contentChecked + 1
  checkSourceMdQuote(
    fc,
    block.text,
    SourceQuoteQuery{
      file: c.file, lineNum: lineNum, actualLine: actualLine,
      endOffset: c.endOffset, lines: res.lines,
    },
  )
}

// Every symbol-adjacent `.bit` citation, and every `<doc>.md:NNN` one, in
// `fc.path`'s own `//` comments, checked.
fn scanSourceFile(fc: SourceFileCtx) {
  let full = "${fc.root}/${fc.path}"
  let text = readFile(full) catch e {
    die("cannot read ${full}: ${e.message()}")
    ""
  }
  if (len(text) > maxCitedFileBytes) {
    die("${fc.path} is ${len(text)} bytes, over the ${maxCitedFileBytes} bound")
  }
  let lines = split(text, "\n")
  if (len(lines) > maxCitedFileLines) {
    die("${fc.path} has ${len(lines)} lines, over the ${maxCitedFileLines} bound")
  }
  for block of extractCommentBlocks(lines) {
    let dbPositions = doubleBacktickPositions(block.text)
    for tok of extractPathTokens(block.text, "") {
      checkSourceToken(fc, block, tok, dbPositions)
    }
    for c of extractMdCitations(block.text) {
      checkSourceMdCitation(fc, block, c, dbPositions)
    }
  }
}

// Scans every `.bit` file under sourceRoots()'s own `//` comments into `sc`;
// returns (filesScanned, citationsFound) so a zero-scan run can't hide in
// the aggregate — the same discipline checkDocument already uses for a
// single DocRow.
fn scanSourceComments(sc: Scan, root: string): (int, int) {
  let index = buildIndexMulti(root, sourceRoots())
  if (len(index) > maxSourceFiles) {
    die("source citation scan covers ${len(index)} file(s), over the ${maxSourceFiles} bound")
  }
  // Once per scan, not once per citation: sourceCheckCtx builds a fresh
  // synthetic row per token and would otherwise re-walk every root each time.
  let declIndex = declarerIndex(root, sourceRoots(), index)
  let beforeCitations = sc.citations
  for path of index {
    scanSourceFile(
      SourceFileCtx{ sc: sc, root: root, index: index, declIndex: declIndex, path: path },
    )
  }
  checkDeclarerPopulation(sc, len(index), len(declIndex))
  if (sc.possessivePaired < minPossessivePairedFloor) {
    report(
      sc,
      "the possessive rule paired and verified only ${sc.possessivePaired} (file, symbol) citation(s), want >= ${minPossessivePairedFloor} — the forward association regressed",
    )
  }
  return len(index), sc.citations - beforeCitations
}
